Since `TimestampFormatter` is a **static class**, it can't receive constructor-injected dependencies. You have two good options:

---

### Option 1 — Convert to a non-static class (recommended)

Make it an instance class and inject `TimeProvider`:

```csharp
public class TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string Format()
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Register it in DI:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
builder.Services.AddSingleton<TimestampFormatter>();
```

Test with `FakeTimeProvider` (`Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet):
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 2, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var formatter = new TimestampFormatter(fake);
Assert.Equal("2026-07-02T00:00:00.0000000+00:00", formatter.Format());
```

---

### Option 2 — Keep it static (ambient context)

If too many call sites depend on the static API, use an `AsyncLocal`-based override:

```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<TimeProvider?> s_override = new();

    private static TimeProvider Clock
        => s_override.Value ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public static string Format()
        => Clock.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");

    // Test-only seam
    public static IDisposable OverrideClock(TimeProvider provider)
    {
        s_override.Value = provider;
        return new Scope();
    }

    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

---

**Recommendation:** Option 1. A non-static class with DI is simpler, idiomatic, and fully testable. Option 2 works but adds complexity and a test-only API surface. Either way, replace every `DateTime.UtcNow` call with `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()`.
